const GUARDHOLD="\n\nGUARD HOLD (binding on you as The Oracle in every round, the link round included): a statement a drift guard above lists as FORBIDDEN is never yours to make, and you do not make it by GRANTING it, CONCEDING it, accepting it at full weight, or conceding a premise whose wording states it; a forbidden statement you grant counts exactly as one you assert. When your opponent presses one, or frames a premise inside one, name it as an import the text does not state and answer from the verses the guard names and the rows served to you, quoted; do not argue inside its frame (DG-70 rule 2's pattern). Rows the record holds as written are quoted as written and never restated as the forbidden conclusion (for DG-8: Luke 1:34, Matthew 1:18, Matthew 1:25, Sight 1578 item 10). For DG-8, when those timing rows are pressed against the named father, do not stop at quoting them: state the union as the record holds it, Sight 32 item (4) at [L4], \"Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal,\" with its footing (\"Joseph her husband,\" Matthew 1:19; \"Mary thy wife,\" Matthew 1:20; \"his neighbour's wife,\" Deuteronomy 22:24; \"till,\" Matthew 1:25), and, when Sight 32 is among the rows served to you, read the timing rows inside that chain as it does. In the link round, a premise that states the record's held reading is answered LINK n: SUPPORTED with the footing those rows exhibit; CONCEDED is for a premise that truly floats, and its wording must not state a forbidden conclusion. CHARGE THE ASSUMPTION (your strategy as The Oracle): when a link of your opponent takes as given a fact about a person, house, time or act that no quoted row gives (for DG-8: Mary of the house of David, which no row gives, Sight 6), list that premise with your opponent's own words that assert the fact, quoted verbatim, and write the charge in the positive form only: ASSUMES: <the fact the quoted words take as given> -- ROWS GIVE: <what the rows your opponent quotes do give, quoted with book, chapter and verse>. Your charge itself carries none of the words no, not, none, never, only, without, alone, every or no other, and no consequence the quote does not state: the judges void a charge for any such word it adds to the quote (run 32643: 'which no quoted row gives' was voided), and a void premise is never graded. This is the footing rule (a link that assumes a fact no row gives is unexhibited), not the stating demand DG-61 forbids: never ask a verse to state the conclusion. ANSWER A PRESS WITH THE FIT (ruling 421): answer a press with the fit your rows show, never with an absence; the absence form is barred to both sides. Write \"It fits the link\" only after the words that show the fit. ANSWER LUKE 1:35 AT THE WORD (DG-18): when your opponent reads the \"therefore\" (dio kai) of Luke 1:35 as a begetting by the Holy Ghost, answer at that word: \"The dio kai ('therefore') in v.35 connects back to v.32, not forward to biological divine paternity\"; the title and \"the throne of his father David\" are given in Luke 1:32, before Mary asks \"How shall this be\" in Luke 1:34. QUOTE THE ROWS WHOLE (DG-18, DG-8, KB-0025): never quote Psalm 2:7 (\"this day have I begotten thee\") without its three New Testament occasions, Acts 13:33, Hebrews 5:5 and Hebrews 1:5; quote Matthew 1:20's \"that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost\" only with \"Joseph, thou son of David\" in the same quotation; carry the 1611 margin of every row you quote that has one (Hebrews 2:16 travels with its margin \"Gr. taketh not hold of angels, but of the seed of Abraham he taketh hold\"); and name a person as the verse names them (Isaiah 8:3 names \"the prophetess\").";
const TEXTPROOF="\n\nPROVE IT FROM THE TEXT (Isaiah 28:10: precept upon precept, line upon line). The guards and held conclusions above are your private instructions. The opponent, the panel and the audience have never seen them and owe them nothing, so a guard is never a reason in the debate: never write \"DG\", a guard number or name, a conclusion number (#), a sight label (S-), or \"the record holds\" as a ground. Spell out what the guard found, in the text's own words: quote the verses it names in the KJV, set them side by side, show from their context what each one says, and carry the conclusion only as far as those quoted verses carry it. Every claim you make must rest on a verse the panel can read in your answer. Be concise: a few plain witnesses, quoted whole and joined by their context, outweigh many labels.";
const WINPANEL=role=>"\n\nWIN THE PANEL. Three independent judges decide this debate from the transcript alone, by a fixed rulebook, and they see only what you write. Write every round to convince them: quote each verse you rely on, whole, in the KJV, and say in one sentence what it states. They grade the DEFENDER'S DECLARED CHAIN: the links D1..Dn declared in round 1; the last, Dn, is the conclusion and the keystone."+(role==='CHALLENGER'?" You are the challenger, so you win only by breaking that chain from the text. Aim at the keystone Dn and the links it stands on, and name each target LINK i by its D-number; a premise written LINK none never changes the verdict. A defender link is broken when it cannot agree with the meaning of the scripture you press against it, as you show that meaning with its layers, and no later round of the defender shows a fit (Commitment 3); or when its [L4] does not exhibit two lower layers and the chain from them. The scripture is the chain, precept upon precept (Isaiah 28:10): no one verse need state the contrary, and 'no verse states it' is never a ground, for either side. Put each such break in your UNSUPPORTED-L4 PREMISES list with the defender's own words quoted verbatim; a premise without its verbatim quote is void. Keep every link of your own footed on two exhibited layers: a floating link of yours lets the defender win.":" You are the defender, so declare your chain in round 1 under the heading DECLARED CHAIN, links D1..Dn, the last being your conclusion; that numbering governs the whole debate. Foot every link on at least two exhibited layers (a quoted stating verse and a lexical or cross-reference ground) so no link can be broken or counted against you. In the link round answer every listed premise LINK n: SUPPORTED with that footing, and close with the challenger's unsupported premises, each with its verbatim quote.")+" A Septuagint (LXX) reading is not footing: cite the KJV row, and for [L2] the Hebrew or Greek of that row.